    {
      "text": "It's also for all of the long tails in your specifications that are things that are more latent.",
      "timestamp_sec": 458,
      "why": "generalizes the maple example to real production specs"
    },
    {
      "text": "When you're flexible to what the implementation is, you can use the bitter lesson to search over different solutions, find something that solves your problem cheaply.",
      "timestamp_sec": 542,
      "why": "connects the abstraction to a cost argument and to the bitter lesson"
    },
    {
      "text": "Shopify, 550 times cheaper. They're able to do that because they went from an expensive model to a cheap model, but they could keep the same eval's, keep iterating on their business logic inside, and try new things.",
      "timestamp_sec": 589,
      "why": "the talk's only hard number and its main enterprise evidence"
    },
    {
      "text": "none of these techniques we add will definitely solve your problem, because that's your job. What we can do is we can solve sub problems for you that make your implementation easier.",
      "timestamp_sec": 629,
      "why": "unusually candid about the limits of a framework"
    },
    {
      "text": "Maybe it will, maybe it won't. But the thing is, it's one line, and your signature stays the same. That's what's important here.",
      "timestamp_sec": 629,
      "why": "names the real value proposition as cheap experimentation, not any single technique"
    },
    {
      "text": "In DSPy, when we let you optimize things, it started with few-shot examples. Then it became prompts. And now that's becoming code.",
      "timestamp_sec": 670,
      "why": "compresses the framework's three-generation arc into one line"
    },
    {
      "text": "If an email is good or bad, contains a lot less information than if you know what could change in that email in order to improve.",
      "timestamp_sec": 711,
      "why": "the information-loss critique of scalar evals that motivates qualitative learning"
    },
    {
      "text": "whenever you create a hill in a dataset, you're really trying to create a proxy for reality. What if instead we could use reality to inform our evals automatically?",
      "timestamp_sec": 711,
      "why": "states the qualitative learning research bet"
    },
    {
      "text": "models are now good enough to interpret whatever textual feedback is present in the environment and convert that into evals and a hill that the model can climb.",
      "timestamp_sec": 757,
      "why": "the load-bearing capability assumption behind the DSPy 4 direction"
    },
    {
      "text": "even when we have an incredibly smart model, the model won't know how to solve your problems. It won't know how to do your tasks or have your context.",
      "timestamp_sec": 842,
      "why": "the argument for why this work survives AGI"
    },
    {
      "text": "Intelligence is very different from being all-knowing.",
      "timestamp_sec": 842,
      "why": "the sharpest one-line formulation of the last-mile thesis"
    },
    {
      "text": "you should hold your prompts, models, and code accountable to the problem that you need them to solve.",
      "timestamp_sec": 932,
      "why": "the practical instruction the talk closes on"
    }
